2026-06-22 20:40:03,664 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/chat "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-06-22 20:40:03,665 | AGENT | SecurityCompliance Agent answered: To ensure compliance with the mentioned regulatory requirements, we can implement several security controls based on our existing capabilities. Firstly, since our hosting partner is OuraneCloud, which is SecNumCloud-qualified, we meet the requirement for data hosting exclusively within the EU. Additionally, our infrastructure holding HDS certification through OuraneCloud's operation covers our processing on this infrastructure.

For encryption and pseudonymisation of sensitive data, we can leverage our existing encryption measures: TLS 1.3 for data in transit and AES-256 for data at rest. Moreover, we have pseudonymisation capabilities in place, which will help protect sensitive health data.

To manage access to the data securely, we utilize Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and strict tenant isolation, ensuring that there is no unauthorized access to client corpora. Our audit logging capabilities will also be crucial for tracking any system outputs and accesses, providing a transparent and traceable record of all activities involving personal or health data.

Given our experience with GDPR delivery, especially concerning special-category (Art. 9) health data, we are well-positioned to handle the specific requirements related to health data processing. Our familiarity with frameworks such as CNIL guidance, HDS reference framework, and HAS guidance on AI in healthcare will be beneficial in ensuring compliance.

However, there are associated risks that need to be considered. One of the primary risks is the potential for data breaches despite our best efforts at encryption and access control. The breach notification delay hours are set at 72 hours, which we must adhere to in case of any incident. Another risk involves the complexities of ensuring GDPR compliance across all aspects of our service, including the secure development life cycle (SDLC), vulnerability management, and incident response planning.

To mitigate these risks, we will need to maintain vigilance over our systems, ensure regular security audits and penetration testing are conducted by external CREST-equivalent providers, and have defined remediation SLAs in place for any vulnerabilities found. Continuous training and awareness programs for our staff on GDPR and health data regulations will also be essential.

By leveraging our existing security controls and compliance measures, we can effectively address the regulatory requirements outlined in the tender while minimizing associated risks through proactive management and monitoring.
